ge or very slightly reduce hyperpigmentation because melatonin has antioxidant properties and may interact with melanin‑producing cells. But even there, the effect is indirect and subtle. It’s not how our natural skin colour is determined, and it does not make someone’s baseline skin permanently lighter. It’s not the same as the pigment‑driven system. #### c) The name similarity Honestly, for most people the confusion is simply: *melatonin* sounds like *melanin*. They share the prefix *melan-* (from Greek *melas*, meaning “black, dark”), because both are related to darkness in different ways: melanin = dark pigment, melatonin = hormone influenced by darkness. But the similarity ends there. --- ### So how does melatonin actually “work” in your body, if not on skin colour? - In the evening, the pineal gland secretes melatonin. - Levels rise at night, helping you fall asleep and stay asleep. - When light hits your eyes in the morning, production drops. - It plays no role in pigmentation (in humans), only in your internal body clock. If you ever see information suggesting that melatonin levels control skin tone in people, it’s either a misunderstanding of animal biology or simply a tip‑of‑the‑tongue word mix‑up. I hope that clears things up — let me know if you’d like to dive deeper into either melanin or melatonin.
